# Chantivo Dedicated Server reverse-proxy example: TLS termination + WebSocket passthrough, with the
# two internal-only operational health routes blocked from public exposure. See README.md in this
# directory for what this does and does not cover, and its real preconditions.
#
# /healthz and /readyz are internal operational endpoints (Docker/systemd health checks, the
# installer's own readiness probe, `chantivo-server doctor`) — they must keep answering locally via
# plain http://127.0.0.1:8090/healthz (services/realtime's own listener, never touched by this file),
# but must NOT be reachable by an anonymous request to the public hostname: there is no reason for an
# internet scanner to learn Chantivo's operational status. The matcher/respond pair below MUST stay
# before the reverse_proxy catch-all and MUST NOT wrap it — every other path, and in particular the
# real WebSocket upgrade this whole product depends on, must keep reaching 127.0.0.1:8090 exactly as
# before.
#
# Copy to an ignored runtime Caddyfile, set CHANTIVO_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME (real public DNS name), and
# run Caddy on the same host as (or with network access to) the Chantivo Dedicated Server.

{$CHANTIVO_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME} {
    @blocked_health path /healthz /readyz
    respond @blocked_health 404

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8090
}
